      "blocks": {
        "column_99ViUi": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/01_aedd38f9-03e5-4913-b547-82c4fb5dfc43.jpg",
            "title": "Take It Off Sometimes",
            "text": "<p>Remove your jewelry before workouts or activities that may expose them to water, like swimming or washing hands. While diamonds can resist sweat, the metal settings can be damaged. Always store your jewelry in a dry, safe place to keep it fresh before putting it away. It is also recommended to take off your jewelry before going to bed to prevent any accidental damage while you sleep.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_HTUp8b": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/02_3bffad3f-e75e-444e-aab1-33fc92449556.jpg",
            "title": "Avoid Chemical",
            "text": "<p>Chemicals such as hairspray, chlorine bleach, perfume, and cleaning solutions can cause your metal to change color. It will be wise to save your fine jewelry for the final stage of any styling process. Also, if you're trying to go full on clean and organize mode, remember to remove it temporarily before doing any housework.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_FLTU6q": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/03_692f8bea-abaf-4ebc-88a5-13e255a20b0c.jpg",
            "title": "How To Store",
            "text": "<p>Do your best to avoid exposure to direct sunlight and excessive heat. This means, securing your jewelry, especially silver, in a holder with individually lined compartments to avoid scratches. If you'd rather have your jewelry out on display, remember to clean your trinket trays and ring holders regularly to eliminate dust.</p>"
          }
        },
        "column_6mYQPc": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/04_28450084-0164-4c8a-8ca0-da007c7bd8b1.jpg",
            "title": "Regular Inspection",
            "text": "<p>Have a professional inspect your jewelry every 6–12 months. Pay special attention to your diamonds’ setting, so you don’t lose those little rocks. Diamonds are extremely hard, yes — but the surrounding settings can dislodge over time and regular use.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
